Evelyn’s life at home is highly roboticized. Her routine (if you can call it that, i suspect she doesn’t really have one) is messy and disorganized. Upstairs in the apartment she’s trying to do her tax paperwork, half-making breakfast for everyone, worrying about her father Gong-Gong, keeping her ears open for trouble downstairs, half-trying to listen to Waymond…her total lack of focus on any one thing is a result of her strict programming to be reactionary to all the stimuli in her life. She has no concept of what’s going on either outside or inside of herself.

	Emotionally, Evelyn acts out in defensive:aggressive ways. The way she speaks to Waymond is mocking and dismissive. She insists on asserting herself over Waymond even when he’s trying to lead the conversation for their mutual benefit. When Evelyn goes downstairs to meet Joy and Becky, the first thing out of her mouth is negative (“I only made enough food for three people. Now I will have to cook more.”). Evelyn has completely forgotten Joy is coming with her and Waymond to the IRS to help with translation while Becky takes care of Gong-Gong. I can’t help but wonder how many times Waymond might have reminded Evelyn of this in the days leading up to the appointment, and yet she still forgot. 

Taking everything we know about Evelyn into consideration, it’s clear to me that her main programming is primarily in the area of Hostile Weakness. Evelyn may be taking charge of things in a Hostile Strength way, but she doesn’t exert enough control to be respected by anyone in her family. She blames everyone else for her problems with control (“You know, with our auditor, she’s a terrible person. Targeting Chinese in the community.” and regarding Waymond, “...sometimes I wonder how he would have survived without me.”). Evelyn tries to fix things with no direction or clear plan of action. At her core, Evelyn is unreliable.

In contrast, Waymond’s programming is in the area of Friendly Strength. He is outwardly friendly, considerate, and spoils people with his kindness. Unfortunately, the way Evelyn speaks to Waymond during their initial conversation at the dining room table makes it clear it’s easy to walk all over him, too. While he may exude the qualities of a natural leader, he doesn’t assume that position. A natural phenomenon of the Hostile Weakness:Friendly Strength dialectic is one of acquiescence by the Friendly party. To show kindness is to allow people to act in the ways that are best for them. Since Evelyn is trying to take charge of their lives already, Waymond is not interested in stepping over her sense of control. He hopes that one day she may actually make the best of it. Of course the days of his being a doormat are coming to an end, considering his need for a divorce. He’s given her over twenty years of chances and all she’s proven to him is her incompetence both personally and professionally. It looks like he wants out.

When it comes to Joy, she’s just plain Hostile. Her attitude is one of Hostile Strength concerning Evelyn, attempting to assert dominance despite the hierarchy of control built into their family unit from parents to child. She’s Hostile Weakness in that she knows she’s not OK but that neither is Evelyn. The only sense of stability Joy gets is from Becky, who dotes on Joy starting from the first scene they’re in together. That avalanche of kisses to calm Joy before seeing her mother is to help Joy find some stability in the present moment. It’s only later that we learn such attempts don’t reach the depths of Joy’s being, otherwise there would be no Jobu Tupaki. The only way Joy has been able to find any control in her life is to go beyond the control of her mother’s overreach. 

Think about it: the pattern of abandonment has repeated from one generation to the next. Where Evelyn left Gong-Gong and her mother behind for a chance at the American Dream, Joy has left Evelyn and Waymond for a chance at a dream of her own. Neither Joy nor Evelyn were accepted by their parents because of their choice of partner. Evelyn because Waymond was taking her to another country and leaving behind the responsibilities to her family, Joy because her mother couldn’t stand her attitude or fully accept her sexual identity. 

The Wang’s found their roles in the family unit through their personal emotional histories, societal expectations, and their interactions with one another. The ways each of them developed a sense of self is clear, as are their dysfunctions. It’s so difficult to find a way beyond the modes of thinking programmed into us, especially if we’re never given the opportunity to. The speed that life in our system demands us to operate at almost ensures we don’t have the time to fight back against our programs.

The pressures of life weigh each of us down differently. The way we handle those pressures determines our well being. There’s never any one right or wrong way to handle our stress. What’s more important is that any effort is made to alleviate those feelings, to allow them time to sit and move through us at their own speed. Circuit II reminds us that we have a place in the world. That doesn’t mean it’s the only place we have to occupy.
